1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,940
Lesson 6.2.

2
00:00:02,320 --> 00:00:04,974
Matrix inverse and Lyapunov equations.

3
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:10,257
In the previous lesson we used only the
mean of the equilibrium distribution.

4
00:00:10,637 --> 00:00:13,426
But the fluctuations
also contain information.

5
00:00:13,806 --> 00:00:18,118
The equilibrium covariance is
β⁻¹ A⁻¹: if we measure how the

6
00:00:18,118 --> 00:00:22,429
variables fluctuate, we get the
inverse of the whole matrix.

7
00:00:22,809 --> 00:00:26,411
And with a small change, we
also solve Lyapunov equations.

8
00:00:27,517 --> 00:00:32,292
The inverse protocol sets b =
0, so that the mean is zero.

9
00:00:32,672 --> 00:00:37,295
After equilibrating, analog
multipliers compute each product

10
00:00:37,295 --> 00:00:41,380
x_i x_j, and analog integrators
average it over time.

11
00:00:41,760 --> 00:00:44,466
That is d² multipliers and integrators.

12
00:00:44,846 --> 00:00:48,002
At the end, A⁻¹ is β times
the matrix of averages.

13
00:00:49,117 --> 00:00:50,652
Here comes a surprise.

14
00:00:51,032 --> 00:00:53,861
In the linear system,
cooling reduced the error.

15
00:00:54,241 --> 00:00:58,823
For the inverse it does not: if the
temperature drops, the fluctuations shrink

16
00:00:58,823 --> 00:01:03,347
proportionally, and the relative error
of the covariance is exactly the same.

17
00:01:03,727 --> 00:01:07,893
What matters is how many
independent fluctuations we average,

18
00:01:07,893 --> 00:01:11,376
that is, the window τ
measured in relaxation times.

19
00:01:12,483 --> 00:01:17,475
We simulate a Wishart matrix of dimension
ten, with condition number twenty-two.

20
00:01:17,855 --> 00:01:22,555
The relative error of the inverse is
fifty percent with a window of a hundred

21
00:01:22,555 --> 00:01:27,193
units, twenty-six with three hundred,
and seven percent with three thousand.

22
00:01:27,573 --> 00:01:30,946
Second moments converge much
more slowly than the mean.

23
00:01:31,326 --> 00:01:34,347
The required window is τ
= 4κ d(d + 1) τ_r / ((1 −

24
00:01:34,347 --> 00:01:37,187
P) ε²), so the complexity
is of order d² κ ε⁻²,

25
00:01:37,567 --> 00:01:44,248
against d^ω digitally.

26
00:01:45,350 --> 00:01:46,613
Now let's generalize.

27
00:01:46,993 --> 00:01:50,775
If the drift is a stable
matrix M, not necessarily

28
00:01:50,775 --> 00:01:53,951
symmetric, and the noise has covariance C,

29
00:01:54,331 --> 00:02:01,325
the stationary covariance satisfies
the Lyapunov equation M Σ + Σ Mᵀ = C.

30
00:02:01,705 --> 00:02:04,273
The inverse is the symmetric special case.

31
00:02:04,653 --> 00:02:08,922
With dimension ten, the error of
the Lyapunov solution is twenty-four

32
00:02:08,922 --> 00:02:13,005
percent with a hundred units and
four percent with three thousand.

33
00:02:13,385 --> 00:02:17,393
Digitally, the Bartels–Stewart
algorithm costs d³.

34
00:02:18,517 --> 00:02:22,314
Lyapunov equations appear
in control theory, where

35
00:02:22,314 --> 00:02:26,491
controllability Gramians tell
which states a system can

36
00:02:26,491 --> 00:02:31,428
reach; in stability analysis; and
in steady-state Kalman filters.

37
00:02:31,808 --> 00:02:35,381
A device of coupled oscillators
solves them simply by

38
00:02:35,381 --> 00:02:39,225
letting noise do its work and
measuring the correlations.

39
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:44,438
Second moments are useful for
approximate preconditioners, for

40
00:02:44,438 --> 00:02:48,460
the posterior covariance in
Bayesian inference, which we will

41
00:02:48,460 --> 00:02:52,087
see in lesson 6.6, and for
Gramians and Kalman filters.

42
00:02:52,467 --> 00:02:55,938
In lab nine you will compare
it with digital methods.

43
00:02:57,050 --> 00:02:59,496
Common mistakes: lowering
the temperature to

44
00:02:59,496 --> 00:03:01,775
improve the inverse, which does not help.

45
00:03:02,155 --> 00:03:05,077
Forgetting to set b to zero
or to subtract the mean.

46
00:03:05,457 --> 00:03:09,155
Underestimating the hardware,
which requires d² multipliers.

47
00:03:09,535 --> 00:03:13,980
And using an unstable drift, for
which no stationary covariance exists.

48
00:03:15,083 --> 00:03:18,786
In summary: the equilibrium
covariance is β⁻¹ A⁻¹, so

49
00:03:18,786 --> 00:03:22,419
measuring correlations gives
the inverse, and with a

50
00:03:22,419 --> 00:03:26,541
general drift it gives the
solution of a Lyapunov equation.

51
00:03:26,921 --> 00:03:30,504
Temperature does not change the
relative error; the window does.

52
00:03:30,884 --> 00:03:35,051
The complexity is of order d² κ ε⁻².

53
00:03:36,183 --> 00:03:40,436
To practice: derive the covariance
from the Lyapunov equation.

54
00:03:40,815 --> 00:03:44,345
Explain why the relative error
does not depend on temperature.

55
00:03:44,725 --> 00:03:47,290
Count the multipliers for
dimension one hundred.

56
00:03:47,670 --> 00:03:51,788
And say which noise is needed to
solve a general Lyapunov equation.
